{"name":"March Hare Orange","hex":"#ff750f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"march","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A formal, rhythmic way of walking, used especially by soldiers, by bands, and in ceremonies.","A journey so walked."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To walk with long, regular strides, as a soldier does.","To cause someone to walk somewhere."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/march"},{"term":"hare","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several plant-eating mammals of the genus Lepus, similar to a rabbit, but larger and with longer ears.","The meat from this animal."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move swiftly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hare"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"March Hare","blocks":[{"text":"The March Hare is a character most famous for appearing in the tea party scene in Lewis Carroll's 1865 book Alice's Adventures in Wonderland."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/March-hare.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March_Hare","wikidata":"Q2587090"},{"title":"March","blocks":[{"text":"March is the third month of the year in both the Julian and Gregorian calendars. Its length is 31 days. In the Northern Hemisphere, the meteorological beginning of spring occurs on the first day of March. The March equinox on the 20 or 21 marks the astronomical beginning of spring in the Northern Hemisphere and the beginning of autumn in the Southern Hemisphere, where September is the seasonal equivalent of the Northern Hemisphere's March."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March","wikidata":"Q110"},{"title":"Hare","blocks":[{"text":"Hares are mammals belonging to the genus Lepus. They are herbivores and live solitarily or in pairs. They nest in depressions in the ground called forms, and their young are able to fend for themselves shortly after birth. The genus includes the largest lagomorphs. Most are fast runners with long, powerful hind legs, and large ears that dissipate body heat. Hare species are native to Africa, Eurasia and North America. A hare less than one year old is called a leveret. A group of hares may be called either a husk or a down."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg/330px-Scrub_Hare_%28Lepus_saxatilis%29_close-up_%2830544290256%29_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hare","wikidata":"Q46076"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Alice_in_Wonderland_%281951%29_-_March_Hare.png/500px-Alice_in_Wonderland_